# DNA-AND content · black holes and quasars
# Real seeds for the black-hole and active-galaxy generators: kinds, parts,
# active galaxy types, events, real examples and rules.
# researched: NASA black hole field guide; Wikipedia "Outline of black holes";
#   Antonucci 1993 (AGN unification review); arXiv 2403.17739 (unified model of AGN);
#   Swinburne COSMOS "Active Galactic Nuclei"
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r05-universe.md

# ── Kinds of black hole ──

@blackhole stellar
name: Stellar-mass black hole
real: yes
mass-sol: 3..100
made-by: the collapse of a very heavy star
says: the most common kind, often found feeding on a companion star
example: V404 Cygni

@blackhole intermediate
name: Intermediate-mass black hole
real: yes
mass-sol: 100..100000
made-by: black holes merging, or a dense star cluster collapsing
says: rare and hard to find; candidates are still debated
example: the remnant of the merger GW190521, about 142 Sun masses

@blackhole supermassive
name: Supermassive black hole
real: yes
mass-sol: 1e6..1e10
where: the center of most big galaxies
example: Sagittarius A*, about 4 million Sun masses

@blackhole ultramassive
name: Ultramassive black hole
real: yes
mass-sol: 1e10..
where: the centers of the biggest galaxies, often in galaxy clusters

@blackhole primordial
name: Primordial black hole
real: hypothetical
made-by: dense spots in the very early universe, just after the Big Bang
says: never confirmed; could come in any size

@blackhole spinning
name: Spinning black hole
real: yes
says: a black hole that rotates, dragging space around with it; it has an ergosphere

@blackhole binary
name: Binary black hole
real: yes
says: two black holes orbiting each other, spiraling closer until they merge

# ── Parts ──

@part singularity
name: Singularity
real: yes
says: the center, where all the mass is crushed

@part event-horizon
name: Event horizon
real: yes
says: the edge past which nothing, not even light, can escape; bigger mass, bigger horizon

@part photon-sphere
name: Photon sphere
real: yes
says: a shell where light can circle the black hole

@part shadow
name: Shadow
real: yes
says: the dark disk seen against glowing gas, as in the first photo of a black hole

@part ergosphere
name: Ergosphere
real: yes
says: the region around a spinning black hole where space itself is dragged around; energy can be taken from it

@part accretion-disk
name: Accretion disk
real: yes
says: a spinning disk of gas heated so hot it shines in X-rays

@part corona
name: Corona
real: yes
says: super-hot gas above the disk

@part jets
name: Jets
real: yes
says: beams of matter fired from the poles at nearly the speed of light, shaped by magnetic fields

@part radio-lobes
name: Radio lobes
real: yes
says: huge clouds where jets crash into the gas around a galaxy

@part dusty-torus
name: Dusty torus
real: yes
says: a thick ring of dust around an active galaxy's core that can hide the center from some angles

# ── Active galaxy cores (a feeding supermassive black hole) ──

@active quasar
name: Quasar
real: yes
says: a feeding supermassive black hole so bright it outshines its whole galaxy; seen far across the universe

@active blazar
name: Blazar
real: yes
says: an active core with its jet pointed straight at the viewer; very bright and flickering
kinds: bl-lac, flat-spectrum quasar

@active bl-lac
name: BL Lacertae object
real: yes
says: a blazar with almost no lines in its light

@active seyfert-1
name: Seyfert galaxy, type 1
real: yes
says: a spiral galaxy with a bright core; fast-moving gas is seen directly

@active seyfert-2
name: Seyfert galaxy, type 2
real: yes
says: like type 1, but the dusty torus hides the fast-moving gas
example: NGC 1068 (Messier 77)

@active radio-galaxy
name: Radio galaxy
real: yes
says: usually an elliptical galaxy with huge radio lobes fed by jets
example: Messier 87

@active liner
name: LINER
real: yes
says: a dim active core, the faint end of active galaxies

@active radio-loud
name: Radio-loud core
real: yes
says: an active core with strong jets and lobes

@active radio-quiet
name: Radio-quiet core
real: yes
says: an active core without strong jets

# ── Events ──

@event tidal-disruption
name: Tidal disruption
real: yes
says: a star wanders too close and is torn apart, flaring brightly as it falls in

@event spaghettification
name: Spaghettification
real: yes
says: the stretching of anything falling toward a black hole

@event merger
name: Black hole merger
real: yes
says: two black holes collide and send out gravitational waves
example: GW190521

@event gravitational-waves
name: Gravitational waves
real: yes
says: ripples in space from black holes and neutron stars colliding

@event feeding-flare
name: Feeding flare
real: yes
says: a quiet black hole lights up when gas falls in

# ── Real examples ──

@example sagittarius-a
name: Sagittarius A*
real: yes
type: supermassive
where: the center of the Milky Way
note: about 4 million Sun masses, no detected jet

@example m87
name: M87*
real: yes
type: supermassive
where: the center of Messier 87
note: the first black hole ever photographed (2019), with a jet about 5,000 light-years long

@example v404-cygni
name: V404 Cygni
real: yes
type: stellar
note: feeding on a Sun-like companion star

@example ngc-1068
name: NGC 1068
real: yes
type: seyfert-2
note: where astronomers found the hidden fast-moving gas behind the dust

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule bh-seed
real: rule
says: every black hole is generated live from the world seed; nothing is pre-built

@rule bh-mass-kind
real: rule
says: mass decides the kind: stellar from dead heavy stars, intermediate from mergers, supermassive at galaxy centers

@rule bh-feeding
real: rule
says: a black hole with gas falling in grows a hot disk and may fire jets; a starved one stays dark

@rule bh-view-angle
real: rule
says: one feeding core looks different from different angles: a blazar seen down the jet, a quasar or Seyfert 1 seen from above, a Seyfert 2 seen through the dust

@rule bh-spin-energy
real: rule
says: energy can be drawn from a spinning black hole's ergosphere; advanced civilizations can tap it

@rule bh-danger
real: rule
says: close in, tides tear apart stars, planets and ships; jets sterilize anything in their path

@rule bh-hypothetical
real: rule
says: primordial black holes are hypothetical; the game may generate them, marked as such
